  "excerpt": "[[page 1]]\nSentryLine: Evidence-Grounded Question Answering over Evolving\nDocuments in Oncology Care\nTampu Ravi Kumar1, Gaurav Najpande1, Muhammad Ali Khan2,\nKaneez Zahra Rubab Khakwani2,Karan Kathuria 1,Shorya Azriel Moses 1,\nYuvraj Kalia3,M Bassam Sonbol 2,Irbaz Bin Riaz 2,Vivek Gupta 1\n1Arizona State University, 2Mayo Clinic, 3Jawaharlal Nehru Medical College\n[Project Page] [Video] [Code]\ntraviku2@asu.edu, gnajpand@asu.edu, vgupt140@asu.edu\nAbstract\nOncology care operates at constant pressure\nof absorbing rapidly evolving evidence base in\nbiomedicine. The American Society of Clini-\ncal Oncology (ASCO) addresses this through\nliving guidelines, but the format introduces a\nnew burden: any recommendation can change\nat any point, across multiple versioned doc-\numents. We present SENTRYLINE, a living\nguideline-aware clinical question answering\nsystem. SENTRYLINEretrieves guideline pas-\nsages through a vectorless hierarchical RAG\npipeline and returns a role-specific answer with\ninline citations, factual and temporal verifica-\ntion reports, and drift detection notes that sur-\nface when a guideline has been updated. We\nconstruct ASCOBENCH, a benchmark of 405\nthree-turn conversations across four question\ncategories with gold answers from expert an-\nnotators(clinicians), and use test set to evaluate\nSENTRYLINEagainst five baselines under an\nLLM-as-judge framework. Experiments across\nthree generation backbones show consistent im-\nprovements over four retrieval baselines and\nASCO’s guideline assistant, with particularly\nstrong gains on Reasoning and Role-Specific\nquestions where multi-hop synthesis and regis-\nter adaptation are required.\n1 Introduction\nOncologists face a rapidly evolving evidence base:\nPubMed indexes over 1.5 million new biomedical\narticles a year (González-Márquez et al., 2024), and\none analysis estimated that a primary care provider\nwould need nearly 27 hours a day to follow ev-\nery current guideline (Porter et al., 2023). The\nAmerican Society of Clinical Oncology (ASCO)\naddresses this throughliving guidelines, updated\non a rolling schedule as new evidence emerges\nacross breast, prostate, lung cancer, and multiple\nmyeloma. A guideline update can reverse a prior\nrecommendation entirely: the 2019 revision to\nASCO’s breast cancer biomarker guideline (An-\ndre et al., 2019), incorporating the TAILORx trial,\nchanged the chemotherapy threshold for many pa-\ntients relative to the 2016 version (Harris et al.,\n2016). Clinicians or patients relying on an out-\ndated version receive outdated guidance (Khairnar,\n2025; Liu et al., 2026), a risk compounded by low\nhealth literacy (Holden et al., 2021) and LLM sus-\nceptibility to hallucination in clinical settings (Kim\net al., 2025b).\nWe built SENTRYLINE, a living guideline-aware\nclinical QA system for oncologists and patients\nworking with ASCO1 living guidelines. Existing\nretrieval-augmented tools, including ASCO’s own\nAI assistant, treat each guideline as static: they\ncannot detect superseded recommenda",
